['| `snapshot` | `at, seq, jobs, ...` | Replaces all state. Always the first record of a compacted log |',
 '| `snapshot` | `at, seq, completionSeq, jobs, idem` | Replaces all state. Always the first record of a compacted log. Each job carries every job field. `idem` lists the remembered keys, `[{ key, id, at, status?, result? }]`; `status` and `result` are there for a key whose job has been pruned |'],
['A reader ignores record types it doesn\'t know.',
 'A retry\'s due time is `at + delayMs` of its `fail` or `expire` record, so every reader and every replay computes the same time. A record about an attempt (`heartbeat`, `complete`, `fail`, `expire`, `release`) only counts while its `fence` is the job\'s live attempt. `complete` numbers commits by their order in the log (`completionSeq`).\n\nA reader ignores record types it doesn\'t know.'],
['Job ids are `j<n>`',
 '## Damage and unfinished writes\n\nOnly the end of the log can hold an unfinished write: an incomplete frame, or a complete frame that fails its checksum with nothing after it. A reader holding the queue lock treats that as never written, and removes it before anything else is appended. Anything else that cannot be decoded raises `CorruptLogError` with its offset and the file is left untouched. A record may not exceed the payload limit; the writer refuses it before writing.\n\nJob ids are `j<n>`'],
]);
edit('docs/compaction.md', [
['1. Build the snapshot from the current state, dropping finished jobs past `retainCompletedMs`.\n2. Write the header and snapshot to `wal.log.compact`.\n3. Rename it over `wal.log`.\n4. Sync, and rebuild this process\'s state from the snapshot.',
 '1. Build the snapshot from the current state, dropping finished jobs past `retainCompletedMs`.\n2. Remove any `wal.log.compact` left by a compaction that died (we hold the lock, so it is nobody\'s), then write the header and snapshot to a fresh one and sync it.\n3. Rename it over `wal.log`. Windows refuses while anyone has the log open, so a refused rename is tried a few more times over a fraction of a second. If it still fails the temp file is removed and the compaction fails; the log is untouched and the next compaction starts clean.\n4. Rebuild this process\'s state from the snapshot.\n\nThe temp file is durable before the rename, so a power cut can never leave an empty log.'],
['The jobs that are kept, and the counters the log needs to carry on.',
 'The jobs that are kept with all their fields (attempts, tokens, due times and so on), the counters the log needs to carry on, and the idempotency keys still within their time to live, including keys whose job was dropped.'],
['A process that was reading the old log finds a different file next time it catches up, and has to start again from the new one.',
 'A process that was reading the old log finds a different generation in the header next time it catches up, and starts again from the new one, whatever the sizes of the two files.\n\n## When it runs by itself\n\nPast `compactAfterBytes`, and once the log has grown by at least the size of its snapshot, so that a live state larger than the threshold is not rewritten on every write.'],
['After any write, automatically, when the log has passed `compactAfterBytes`.','After any write, automatically, when the log has passed `compactAfterBytes` (see below).'],
]);
